" :answer-field/choices ["q-328350/field-4/3202c38f69df9ba7e6146205db4b06907313ea52812cc80593d5589029147966"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328350" :question/id #uuid "2ccfedc3-8e1d-5dac-9606-34084cd890a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-328350" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in radians, the **exact solutions** to the equation  $3tan\\,(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3}=0,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,{x}_{3}=$\n{{field-3}}$,\\,{x}_{4}=$\n{{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})-3\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n2x+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(6n+1)\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ \n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{19\\pi }{12}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-328350/field-1" "q-328350/field-2" "q-328350/field-3" "q-328350/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3bd5e5c-b8d1-53e5-9968-8bef8faceb4b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-328350"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-328369/field-1/22544889ddd7b88350edc45f9a186dfd49afc624841b8647f5c31e52408e5538" :answer/id #uuid "1eb68341-b796-5347-a7b8-8e1c6144b5aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-180" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-328369/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "bac141b0-1322-5b9e-bf4e-e58b94ef95c9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-328369/field-1/22544889ddd7b88350edc45f9a186dfd49afc624841b8647f5c31e52408e5538" :answer-field/choices ["q-328369/field-1/22544889ddd7b88350edc45f9a186dfd49afc624841b8647f5c31e52408e5538"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328369/field-2/f4ed4b34f4a5de49898377314514d2de061007c11b4d8d545eea74864f386ee8" :answer/id #uuid "d501dd71-637d-5a79-9da9-43a3412b618f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-328369/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "5b3c713c-136a-5aca-ae53-2b5eb3571ad8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-328369/field-2/f4ed4b34f4a5de49898377314514d2de061007c11b4d8d545eea74864f386ee8" :answer-field/choices ["q-328369/field-2/f4ed4b34f4a5de49898377314514d2de061007c11b4d8d545eea74864f386ee8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-328369" :question/id #uuid "aa7852c7-812a-5d97-bd66-542840db60ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-328369" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the **exact solutions** to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x+{45}^{∘})=1,$ for $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<0,$  are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{45}^{∘} & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-{45}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ 2x & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =n\\cdot {90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<0.$\n\n- Setting $n=-2$ gives $x=-{180}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-{90}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-{180}^{∘}\\le x<0.$ \n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={-180}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={-90}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-328369/field-1" "q-328369/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9eb12a99-5169-52f7-8830-5de0d2d854f6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-328369"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
